    "age_title=For your Fake Currency Detection using Deep Learning project, you can create a simple user interface where the user uploads a currency image and the system predicts whether it is Real or Fake using your trained deep learning model.\n",
    "I recommend using Python + Streamlit because it is easy to connect with your model and suitable for a college project.\n",
    "1. User interface design\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "fake Currency Detection\n",
    "AI-powered Indian banknote image analysis\n",
    "\n",
    "Upload Currency Image\n",
    "Choose a currency image\n",
    "Upload a JPG, JPEG, or PNG image\n",
    "\n",
    "Prediction result\n",
    "Your result will appear here\n",
    "The model will analyze the uploaded image.\n",
    "\n",
    "Demo layout only. Actual predictions require a trained model.\n",
    "\n",
    "2. Install the required libraries\n",
    "Run this in a Jupyter Notebook cell:\n",
    "!pip install streamlit tensorflow pillow numpy\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "3. Create the user interface code\n",
    "Create a Python file named app.py and add this code. It provides an upload interface and connects to a saved TensorFlow/Keras model.\n",
    "import streamlit as stimport tensorflow as tfimport numpy as npfrom PIL import Imagest.set_page_config(    p\"Fake Currency Detection\",    page_icon=\"💵\",    layout=\"centered\")st.title(\"💵 Fake Currency Detection\")st.write(    \"Upload an Indian currency image \"    \"to analyze it using deep learning.\")@st.cache_resourcedef load_model():    return tf.keras.models.load_model(\"currency_model.keras\")uploaded_file = st.file_uploader(    \"Upload currency image\",    type=[\"jpg\", \"jpeg\", \"png\"])if uploaded_file is not None:    image = Image.open(uploaded_file).convert(\"RGB\")    st.image(image, caption=\"Uploaded Currency\",             use_container_width=True)\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "Important: This example assumes a binary classification model with a single sigmoid output, where 0 = Real and 1 = Fake. Your training code must use the same image size, normalization, and class mapping. If your model produces two output probabilities, the prediction code needs to be adjusted.\n",
    "4. Run the interface\n",
    "Open a terminal in the folder containing app.py and your saved model, then run:\n",
    "streamlit run app.py\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "Your browser will open the web interface.\n",
    "5. Project folder structure\n",
    "FakeCurrencyDetection/\n",
    "│\n",
    "├── app.py\n",
    "├── currency_model.keras\n",
